blitz-config
============

Native configurator for the Cosmic Byte Blitz controller, written in
Rust with egui. It is a single binary (~6 MB) that links only against
libc and libudev.

It does what Cosmic Byte's Orbit web configurator does, without a
browser or their servers:

  - button remapping
  - stick and trigger ranges, radial trace, D-pad swap, diagonal lock
  - vibration levels and motor tests
  - gyro: activation, output, deadzone, response curve, axes,
    calibration
  - auto-sleep and pick-up-to-wake
  - four named profiles
  - live input preview

Unofficial. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Cosmic Byte. This tool
writes to your controller's settings; use it at your own risk.


Building and running
--------------------

    cargo install blitz-config

or, from a checkout:

    cargo build --release
    ./target/release/blitz-config

Pass --dry-run to connect and read normally without sending anything
that changes the controller.

Connect the controller over wired USB (0281:0080) or the 2.4 GHz dongle
(0281:0112), in DInput mode. Bluetooth and XInput mode do not expose
the configuration channel.

Close any browser tab that is using the controller first. Two
configurators sending commands to one controller at once is unsafe.

On Linux, give your user access to the device once, then replug it:

    sudo cp 70-cosmic-byte-blitz.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
    sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger


Accuracy and safety
-------------------

The controller's settings are a 1117-byte profile, written in 55-byte
chunks over a vendor HID channel. A wrong byte goes straight into the
controller's flash, so the following measures are in place.

Differential tests
    tools/gen-vectors.mjs runs the Orbit web configurator's own code
    on a fake DOM and a fake HID device: its profile setters, its
    slider and curve-drag widgets, its reset handler and its driver.
    It records the resulting bytes and reports in tests/vectors.json.
    'cargo test' requires this implementation to reproduce all of them
    exactly: 1,000 edit sequences, 250 write plans, and the read,
    session, rumble, calibration, profile-switch and firmware-parsing
    cases.

Descriptor check
    A device is only opened if its HID report descriptor declares the
    vendor config collection (usage page 0xFFF0, OUT report 0x0F).

Complete reads only
    A profile only becomes editable once all 21 chunks have arrived.
    Reads have no side effects and are retried. Writes are never
    retried.

Backups
    Every profile read is saved to
    ~/.local/share/blitz-config/backups/ before it can be edited.

Read-back verification
    Each write is read back and compared byte for byte. On a mismatch,
    auto-apply stops and the editor shows what the controller actually
    holds.

Audit log
    Every report sent and every config reply received is appended to
    ~/.local/share/blitz-config/hid.log.

Dry run
    --dry-run logs writes, rumble, calibration and profile switches
    instead of sending them.

Single session
    Only one blitz-config window can be connected at a time.

A read-only hardware check, which runs in dry-run mode, reads all four
profiles twice and checks that the reads agree:

    cargo run --example probe


Regenerating the test vectors
-----------------------------

The reference web app is not included. To regenerate or extend the
vectors, save your own copy of blitz-configurator.html and
BlitzDriver.js from Orbit into blitz/, then run:

    node tools/gen-vectors.mjs

For a larger one-off run:

    VECTORS_CASES=20000 VECTORS_SEED=7 VECTORS_OUT=/tmp/v.json \
        node tools/gen-vectors.mjs
    BLITZ_VECTORS=/tmp/v.json cargo test --release


Deliberate differences from the web app
---------------------------------------

  - If a stored slider range is collapsed (max <= initial), the web app
    pulls the handles apart and saves the result as soon as the page is
    shown. This tool only does so when you drag that slider, so viewing
    a page never changes the controller.

  - Pending edits are written before a profile switch or disconnect
    instead of being dropped.

  - Reads and session start are retried, writes are read back and
    verified, and "Reset to default" asks for confirmation.


Protocol notes
--------------

  Config OUT    report 0x0F, padded to 63 bytes:
                00 00 3C <opcode> <args...>

  Reply IN      report 0x10: 00 00 3C <opcode+1> ...
                Session commands are answered with 0x06.

  Opcodes       01  enter config mode
                02  exit
                03  write a profile span
                04  read a profile span
                07  firmware version
                0C  apply a written span
                E0  LED refresh
                FD  gyro calibration

  Profiles      numbered 1-4 on the wire. Read replies always carry
                01 in byte 5, whichever profile was requested.

  Rumble        report 0x05, exactly five bytes:
                01 00 00 <right> <left>

  Heartbeat     report 0x0F: 54 34 4B 00, sent once a second while
                connected.

  Live input    report 0x01.

The profile layout is documented in src/profile.rs.


